1
00:00:03,430 --> 00:00:04,770
Hello, everyone on Bilibili.

2
00:00:05,110 --> 00:00:10,860
At the strong urging of another
Bilibili creator [name unclear],

3
00:00:10,860 --> 00:00:16,570
I'll demonstrate the
game in this tutorial.

4
00:00:17,750 --> 00:00:23,270
This mainly covers some
official Sanguo Yan Yi Qi rules,

5
00:00:23,270 --> 00:00:28,910
as well as some rules we devised
privately that most of us accept.

6
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:34,050
First, let me briefly explain
what Sanguo Yan Yi Qi is.

7
00:00:34,790 --> 00:00:39,780
It combines Romance of the Three
Kingdoms with Chinese chess,

8
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:43,460
two well-known cultural
elements, in a tabletop game.

9
00:00:44,540 --> 00:00:47,064
Look at the video to see
how the pieces are arranged.

10
00:00:48,610 --> 00:00:51,350
How is the board designed?

11
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:54,650
Wei and Shu face each other directly.

12
00:00:54,710 --> 00:00:59,350
But Wu has no active opposing
force directly across from it.

13
00:00:59,410 --> 00:01:00,770
Which force is opposite Wu?

14
00:01:00,910 --> 00:01:04,010
The Eastern Han. During play,

15
00:01:04,320 --> 00:01:09,930
Han cannot move unless the emperor
is killed or an alliance is formed.

16
00:01:09,930 --> 00:01:15,740
So initially, Wu has no force
directly confronting it. Now look at

17
00:01:15,740 --> 00:01:21,390
the board's layout. Wei occupies Xuchang.

18
00:01:21,860 --> 00:01:27,490
Cao Wei had not yet usurped the Han
throne, so its capital is still Xuchang.

19
00:01:27,490 --> 00:01:33,520
Wu's capital is Jianye. There is
little to add there. Then look at

20
00:01:33,520 --> 00:01:35,300
Shu, whose capital is Chengdu.

21
00:01:36,550 --> 00:01:39,410
It has already taken Western Sichuan.

22
00:01:39,490 --> 00:01:41,830
Liu Bei has already conquered that region.

23
00:01:41,930 --> 00:01:46,070
The emperor's position here, Emperor
Xian of Han, represents Luoyang.

24
00:01:46,460 --> 00:01:50,490
Outside Luoyang is Hulao Pass.

25
00:01:50,570 --> 00:01:53,840
Look, this is Hulao Pass.

26
00:01:56,080 --> 00:01:58,480
Emperor Xian has three
chariots and one cannon.

27
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:01,220
They are stationed here
at Luoyang and Hulao Pass.

28
00:02:02,160 --> 00:02:08,620
Besides its capital, each faction
has five important strongholds.

29
00:02:09,130 --> 00:02:12,710
Here in Wei are Xuzhou and Runan,

30
00:02:13,450 --> 00:02:15,770
Xinye and Wancheng,

31
00:02:16,350 --> 00:02:20,090
and Chang'an. In the first and
second editions of the board,

32
00:02:20,150 --> 00:02:25,150
the two cannon positions also had city
names. From the third edition onward,

33
00:02:25,830 --> 00:02:27,910
those two city names were omitted.

34
00:02:29,170 --> 00:02:31,090
I'm using the sixth-edition board.

35
00:02:31,150 --> 00:02:34,710
The fifth and sixth editions
both use this same board design.

36
00:02:34,770 --> 00:02:36,920
Now look at Wu.

37
00:02:36,980 --> 00:02:38,520
Here is Shouchun,

38
00:02:39,980 --> 00:02:42,130
Lujiang and Chaisang,

39
00:02:43,700 --> 00:02:45,730
Xiangyang and Jiangling.

40
00:02:46,530 --> 00:02:49,430
I don't know exactly what
happened in these cities.

41
00:02:49,530 --> 00:02:53,750
I simply enjoy playing Sanguo Yan Yi Qi.

42
00:02:53,750 --> 00:02:57,179
I'm not very familiar with the stories
in Romance of the Three Kingdoms.

43
00:02:57,750 --> 00:03:02,170
So I can only give you a brief
introduction based on the board's layout.

44
00:03:02,230 --> 00:03:03,870
Here in Shu is Yong'an.

45
00:03:04,150 --> 00:03:07,648
I know about Liu Bei's deathbed
entrustment at Yong'an,

46
00:03:07,648 --> 00:03:09,810
so I'm not a totally ignorant fan!

47
00:03:10,480 --> 00:03:13,110
Then there are Shangyong and Hanzhong.

48
00:03:14,370 --> 00:03:17,230
And Jiange. That is quite a famous place.

49
00:03:17,410 --> 00:03:19,610
I don't know the name
of the place below it.

50
00:03:20,200 --> 00:03:21,920
Now, on to the main topic.

51
00:03:22,950 --> 00:03:25,890
First I'll explain how the pieces move.

52
00:03:26,390 --> 00:03:29,630
Their moves are basically
the same as in Chinese chess,

53
00:03:29,690 --> 00:03:31,830
but there are some differences.

54
00:03:32,440 --> 00:03:36,230
I'll use this black chariot as an example.

55
00:03:36,910 --> 00:03:39,940
The chariot can move horizontally

56
00:03:40,490 --> 00:03:43,150
or vertically, any number of spaces.

57
00:03:43,250 --> 00:03:47,470
For example, it can move
directly from here to here.

58
00:03:48,070 --> 00:03:50,570
Or directly from here to here.

59
00:03:51,490 --> 00:03:54,236
Both are allowed. It captures
an encountered enemy piece,

60
00:03:54,236 --> 00:03:55,585
but cannot jump over pieces.

61
00:03:55,890 --> 00:03:57,860
Now let's introduce the advisor.

62
00:03:59,110 --> 00:04:01,270
Again, I'll use Black's advisor.

63
00:04:01,760 --> 00:04:04,300
Only diagonally, within
the nine-point palace,

64
00:04:04,480 --> 00:04:06,040
just as in Chinese chess.

65
00:04:07,330 --> 00:04:10,210
Now look at the Wei, Shu and Wu pieces.

66
00:04:10,720 --> 00:04:14,149
These are each side's king, equivalent
to the generals in Chinese chess.

67
00:04:14,210 --> 00:04:16,030
They cannot leave their palaces.

68
00:04:16,410 --> 00:04:20,630
But there is a difference from
the generals in Chinese chess.

69
00:04:22,300 --> 00:04:24,680
Wei and Shu may face each other directly.

70
00:04:24,740 --> 00:04:28,060
There is no restriction against that. Why?

71
00:04:28,120 --> 00:04:29,660
If that restriction existed,

72
00:04:29,780 --> 00:04:32,400
whose king would Wu's king be facing?

73
00:04:32,860 --> 00:04:35,240
Right? You can think of it that way.

74
00:04:35,960 --> 00:04:40,120
Of course, these three ruler
pieces, Wei, Shu and Wu,

75
00:04:40,120 --> 00:04:43,320
if one is checkmated, that faction loses.

76
00:04:43,780 --> 00:04:46,300
I'll explain how the
defeated faction is handled

77
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:47,840
later.

78
00:04:49,020 --> 00:04:54,140
Now look at the horse. Like in
Chinese chess, it moves in an L shape.

79
00:04:55,050 --> 00:04:59,630
But unlike Chinese chess, it has
no blocked-horse-leg restriction.

80
00:04:59,830 --> 00:05:04,170
For example, suppose the
position looks like this.

81
00:05:04,230 --> 00:05:07,620
In Chinese chess, wouldn't
this horse be unable to move?

82
00:05:07,680 --> 00:05:10,360
Here, however, it can still jump out.

83
00:05:13,280 --> 00:05:16,180
The horse also has another important role,

84
00:05:16,240 --> 00:05:19,990
which I'll explain later.
Now let's look at the cannon.

85
00:05:21,270 --> 00:05:24,730
The cannon works much the same way,

86
00:05:24,790 --> 00:05:30,030
but this game restricts cannons
in the very first round.

87
00:05:30,410 --> 00:05:34,410
It cannot move beyond the defensive line,

88
00:05:34,490 --> 00:05:36,870
which runs through the five cities.

89
00:05:37,070 --> 00:05:39,530
All five soldiers are
stationed on that line.

90
00:05:39,670 --> 00:05:42,050
The cannon cannot go beyond it.

91
00:05:42,110 --> 00:05:45,240
It can only move sideways, or
one space forward or backward.

92
00:05:45,660 --> 00:05:48,840
Why? If it could move out
on its very first turn,

93
00:05:49,780 --> 00:05:53,920
let me just arrange this example...
If it could go out immediately,

94
00:05:53,980 --> 00:05:56,760
wouldn't Wu be certain to lose a chariot?

95
00:05:59,240 --> 00:06:04,820
So the cannon's restriction gives
Wu a first move to defend itself.

96
00:06:04,820 --> 00:06:10,670
Wu may shift a cannon, jump a horse,
or move a soldier sideways.

97
00:06:10,670 --> 00:06:16,813
It gets that one defensive move.
That's why the cannon is restricted.

98
00:06:16,813 --> 00:06:17,970
We've covered

99
00:06:17,970 --> 00:06:23,500
the chariot, horse, cannon and advisor.
Next is the elephant. Like the horse,

100
00:06:23,500 --> 00:06:29,090
in Chinese chess it has a blocking rule:
its elephant's eye can be blocked.

101
00:06:29,090 --> 00:06:33,710
That looks like this. If this were

102
00:06:34,280 --> 00:06:36,860
Chinese chess, wouldn't the
elephant be unable to move?

103
00:06:36,880 --> 00:06:39,780
But here it can still jump across.

104
00:06:40,230 --> 00:06:43,390
That restriction is absent.
What is the purpose?

105
00:06:43,450 --> 00:06:46,260
To strengthen defence, because
this is a three-player game.

106
00:06:46,730 --> 00:06:49,630
If we retained the
blocked-elephant's-eye restriction,

107
00:06:49,690 --> 00:06:51,833
it would be very
disadvantageous for defence.

108
00:06:53,090 --> 00:06:55,430
So, to strengthen defence,

109
00:06:55,490 --> 00:06:58,160
the elephant cannot have its eye blocked.

110
00:06:58,220 --> 00:07:00,800
There is one more point
about the elephant.

111
00:07:00,920 --> 00:07:06,350
Within its own territory, it may only
move among its seven home points.

112
00:07:07,140 --> 00:07:09,620
Why am I pointing this out?

113
00:07:09,680 --> 00:07:12,460
When I first started playing this game,

114
00:07:13,350 --> 00:07:15,850
an opponent and I once
reached a position like this.

115
00:07:15,910 --> 00:07:18,481
I asked, "Can these two
elephants capture each other?"

116
00:07:19,000 --> 00:07:22,000
They said no, because these elephants

117
00:07:22,340 --> 00:07:24,600
are both on their national boundaries.

118
00:07:24,660 --> 00:07:27,260
They are facing each other,

119
00:07:27,420 --> 00:07:30,320
but cannot capture each
other across the boundary.

120
00:07:32,000 --> 00:07:33,280
There is, however,

121
00:07:33,560 --> 00:07:35,640
an extra elephant rule I'll explain later.

122
00:07:35,700 --> 00:07:37,033
It also applies to advisors.

123
00:07:38,700 --> 00:07:41,140
Having introduced those main pieces,

124
00:07:41,200 --> 00:07:43,940
let's look at the soldier. Its moves

125
00:07:43,980 --> 00:07:47,980
differ quite a lot from Chinese chess.

126
00:07:48,020 --> 00:07:51,000
It can move sideways
from its very first move.

127
00:07:51,060 --> 00:07:54,460
Like this. It can move
sideways immediately.

128
00:07:54,520 --> 00:07:56,040
Look at that sideways move.

129
00:07:56,060 --> 00:07:59,020
If these soldiers form a connected row,

130
00:07:59,080 --> 00:08:01,520
don't they make a very strong defence?

131
00:08:01,520 --> 00:08:04,396
That is the original designer's intention:

132
00:08:04,396 --> 00:08:07,340
to have soldiers
defending along this line.

133
00:08:07,360 --> 00:08:10,760
Why call it a defensive line rather
than the pawn or soldier rank?

134
00:08:11,110 --> 00:08:15,690
Because the soldiers can form
a very powerful defence there.

135
00:08:18,000 --> 00:08:21,360
On the defensive line, soldiers
may move sideways but not backward.

136
00:08:21,880 --> 00:08:26,480
They cannot retreat. One step forward
puts them on their national boundary.

137
00:08:27,320 --> 00:08:29,844
The boundary is one line
ahead of the defensive line.

138
00:08:30,330 --> 00:08:31,990
Here is Wei's national boundary,

139
00:08:32,150 --> 00:08:33,630
Wu's national boundary,

140
00:08:34,000 --> 00:08:35,440
and Shu's national boundary.

141
00:08:36,730 --> 00:08:39,330
On the boundary, it moves
as on the defensive line.

142
00:08:39,390 --> 00:08:43,410
It may move forward or sideways, but
cannot retreat to the defensive line.

143
00:08:44,170 --> 00:08:46,360
But once it goes beyond
the national boundary,

144
00:08:47,330 --> 00:08:50,270
it may move forward, sideways or backward.

145
00:08:50,530 --> 00:08:53,006
It can retreat only as far
as the national boundary.

146
00:08:53,270 --> 00:08:58,330
The same applies if it
enters enemy territory.

147
00:08:58,810 --> 00:09:02,280
In this position, imagine
these advisors are absent,

148
00:09:02,340 --> 00:09:04,520
leaving Shu with only its king.

149
00:09:05,140 --> 00:09:10,300
The soldier can still threaten that
king up on the palace's top edge.

150
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:16,380
Having explained how the pieces move,

151
00:09:16,560 --> 00:09:18,420
I'll now introduce the Eastern Han.

152
00:09:18,960 --> 00:09:22,077
Before the emperor is
killed or an alliance forms,

153
00:09:22,077 --> 00:09:24,570
Han's pieces cannot move or be captured.

154
00:09:25,030 --> 00:09:29,410
But they may be used as
obstacles or as cannon screens.

155
00:09:29,450 --> 00:09:30,879
For example, put a cannon here

156
00:09:31,320 --> 00:09:33,740
and Wu's chariot here.
The cannon can capture it.

157
00:09:34,200 --> 00:09:37,724
But the chariot cannot capture the
cannon because the Han piece blocks it.

158
00:09:40,250 --> 00:09:43,990
As for the Han emperor,

159
00:09:44,090 --> 00:09:47,950
I'll explain him shortly.
Now let's discuss turn order.

160
00:09:48,390 --> 00:09:52,600
When I explained the cannon,

161
00:09:52,780 --> 00:09:54,018
I already touched on that.

162
00:09:54,300 --> 00:09:56,320
That was the issue with the cannon.

163
00:09:56,380 --> 00:09:58,660
There is a similar
concern with turn order:

164
00:09:58,720 --> 00:10:00,100
we also have to consider

165
00:10:00,160 --> 00:10:04,040
preventing Shu and Wei from attacking
Wu together before it can act.

166
00:10:04,100 --> 00:10:06,080
So Wu moves first.

167
00:10:06,140 --> 00:10:08,360
After Wu's move,

168
00:10:08,420 --> 00:10:10,480
play proceeds counterclockwise:

169
00:10:10,520 --> 00:10:11,980
Wei, then Shu,

170
00:10:12,040 --> 00:10:13,740
then Wu again, in that order.

171
00:10:13,800 --> 00:10:15,480
Wei and Shu follow in sequence.

172
00:10:17,960 --> 00:10:23,820
But if a faction is checked—for
example, Wei checks Wu—

173
00:10:23,820 --> 00:10:26,520
Wu is the player immediately before Wei.

174
00:10:27,070 --> 00:10:31,030
When a player is in check, another rule
applies: the checked player moves first.

175
00:10:32,030 --> 00:10:35,370
A checked faction must
respond immediately.

176
00:10:35,430 --> 00:10:37,192
For example, Wu adds an advisor here.

177
00:10:38,010 --> 00:10:40,130
After Wu has moved and answered the check,

178
00:10:40,190 --> 00:10:42,970
its next player still moves. That is Wei.

179
00:10:43,030 --> 00:10:46,470
Wei can make another move
with some other piece.

180
00:10:47,440 --> 00:10:49,480
So Sanguo Yan Yi Qi

181
00:10:49,540 --> 00:10:55,250
has this principle of play:

182
00:10:55,250 --> 00:10:59,890
players are encouraged to attack
the player immediately before them.

183
00:10:59,990 --> 00:11:01,140
Now,

184
00:11:01,670 --> 00:11:03,410
I'll explain alliances,

185
00:11:03,470 --> 00:11:05,930
and alliances versus
killing the Han emperor.

186
00:11:06,830 --> 00:11:12,190
Around Emperor Xian, you can see three
alliance points marked Wei, Wu and Shu.

187
00:11:13,060 --> 00:11:17,280
For alliances and killing the emperor,
one piece is particularly important:

188
00:11:17,340 --> 00:11:21,380
the horse. In this game,

189
00:11:21,440 --> 00:11:24,440
the horse has a very
important strategic role.

190
00:11:24,860 --> 00:11:27,990
To ally with another faction,

191
00:11:28,050 --> 00:11:29,710
send one of your horses over.

192
00:11:30,150 --> 00:11:31,864
For example, Wei's horse jumps here.

193
00:11:32,170 --> 00:11:38,210
For an alliance with Shu, jump to the
Shu point; with Wu, jump to the Wu point.

194
00:11:38,210 --> 00:11:43,850
But note: jumping onto the point
marked with your own faction

195
00:11:43,850 --> 00:11:49,390
does not have any special effect.

196
00:11:49,390 --> 00:11:54,990
You may move there, but it
does nothing. There is also

197
00:11:54,990 --> 00:11:56,419
another point about alliances.

198
00:11:57,320 --> 00:11:59,720
It does not matter whether
the other player agrees,

199
00:11:59,780 --> 00:12:01,360
or whether it was intentional.

200
00:12:01,420 --> 00:12:04,100
For example, while
repositioning my forces,

201
00:12:04,160 --> 00:12:07,240
I accidentally move my
horse onto the Shu point.

202
00:12:07,450 --> 00:12:09,750
Even if Shu is currently attacking Wei,

203
00:12:09,810 --> 00:12:11,920
the moment the horse jumps here,

204
00:12:12,140 --> 00:12:15,160
the alliance takes effect
and cannot be retracted.

205
00:12:15,480 --> 00:12:17,840
Nor can the alliance be dissolved at will.

206
00:12:17,900 --> 00:12:20,670
A condition must be met. For example,

207
00:12:21,050 --> 00:12:23,010
Wei and Shu are now allied.

208
00:12:23,110 --> 00:12:25,090
Move the Han marker to the Wu position.

209
00:12:26,400 --> 00:12:28,160
That indicates a Wei–Shu alliance.

210
00:12:28,200 --> 00:12:32,900
The yellow Han forces, three chariots
and one cannon, all belong to Wu.

211
00:12:32,960 --> 00:12:36,780
Wei and Shu may not capture
each other's pieces.

212
00:12:36,840 --> 00:12:39,160
They can only attack Wu.

213
00:12:39,220 --> 00:12:42,900
Wu now has two powerful opponents,

214
00:12:42,960 --> 00:12:44,920
but also receives additional forces.

215
00:12:44,980 --> 00:12:49,660
So it will not be too weak by comparison.

216
00:12:50,680 --> 00:12:54,980
When the two allies eliminate Wu,

217
00:12:55,780 --> 00:12:57,860
Only then does it dissolve automatically.

218
00:12:57,920 --> 00:13:00,180
Or if Wu eliminates
either one of the allies,

219
00:13:00,530 --> 00:13:03,170
the alliance also dissolves automatically.

220
00:13:05,950 --> 00:13:08,664
Besides alliances, there is
also killing the Han emperor.

221
00:13:08,870 --> 00:13:12,510
Again, I'll use Wei as an example.

222
00:13:12,590 --> 00:13:15,700
Suppose Wei captures the Han emperor.

223
00:13:16,410 --> 00:13:19,130
Put the Han piece in the
position of Wei's ruler.

224
00:13:20,190 --> 00:13:22,090
You may remove the original ruler piece,

225
00:13:22,150 --> 00:13:23,670
replacing your own ruler.

226
00:13:23,730 --> 00:13:28,030
It still represents you;
its role is still Wei's king.

227
00:13:29,540 --> 00:13:35,824
The three yellow chariots and cannon
now belong to the faction of that black

228
00:13:35,824 --> 00:13:39,560
horse. The other two factions
immediately form an alliance,

229
00:13:39,620 --> 00:13:41,420
automatically, to oppose it.

230
00:13:42,380 --> 00:13:46,880
As allies, those two may not
capture each other's pieces.

231
00:13:47,020 --> 00:13:49,734
They may only attack the
faction that killed the emperor.

232
00:13:50,060 --> 00:13:56,080
Keep that in mind. As for

233
00:13:56,080 --> 00:13:59,540
Han's role in balancing the forces,

234
00:13:59,620 --> 00:14:03,320
I have one particular addition to suggest.

235
00:14:04,510 --> 00:14:08,050
Suppose neither an alliance nor the
killing of the emperor has occurred.

236
00:14:09,020 --> 00:14:16,020
Wei then checkmates Wu, and Wei takes over

237
00:14:20,410 --> 00:14:21,970
Wu's remaining pieces.

238
00:14:22,220 --> 00:14:23,960
More on taking over pieces later.

239
00:14:24,020 --> 00:14:25,639
For now, let me give this example.

240
00:14:26,370 --> 00:14:29,140
Shu still has only its original forces,

241
00:14:29,200 --> 00:14:31,860
while Wei now has a powerful army.

242
00:14:32,400 --> 00:14:34,560
If Wei then kills the Han emperor as well,

243
00:14:34,620 --> 00:14:37,560
wouldn't the battle become
completely one-sided?

244
00:14:38,360 --> 00:14:42,480
So, with the balance of forces in mind,

245
00:14:43,560 --> 00:14:50,560
when I first learned the game, I made
this suggestion to the official designers:

246
00:14:51,100 --> 00:14:56,670
if one side checkmates another before
an alliance or the emperor's death,

247
00:14:56,670 --> 00:15:02,360
could we give Han directly to the
third faction to reinforce it?

248
00:15:02,360 --> 00:15:07,990
In other words, the
moment Wei checkmates Wu,

249
00:15:07,990 --> 00:15:13,970
Han activates and its three
chariots and cannon go to Shu.

250
00:15:13,970 --> 00:15:16,110
That would happen even without

251
00:15:16,940 --> 00:15:18,900
an alliance or killing the emperor.

252
00:15:19,200 --> 00:15:20,960
Han would activate automatically,

253
00:15:21,020 --> 00:15:24,600
because it exists to balance
the forces on the board.

254
00:15:25,240 --> 00:15:27,760
If those forces become unbalanced,

255
00:15:27,820 --> 00:15:29,400
and Han plays no role,

256
00:15:29,500 --> 00:15:34,120
then including Han as a balancing
force would be entirely pointless.

257
00:15:35,310 --> 00:15:37,030
For that reason,

258
00:15:37,090 --> 00:15:39,330
I suggested adding this rule.

259
00:15:39,390 --> 00:15:41,390
The officials said they would consider it.

260
00:15:46,690 --> 00:15:48,880
Now let's discuss taking
over defeated armies.

261
00:15:50,550 --> 00:15:53,520
There is quite a lot to say about this.

262
00:15:55,010 --> 00:15:56,170
First of all,

263
00:15:56,930 --> 00:16:02,490
when one faction checkmates another,

264
00:16:02,490 --> 00:16:08,900
all the defeated faction's surviving
pieces belong to the victor.

265
00:16:08,900 --> 00:16:15,370
If Wei checkmates Wu, all
Wu's pieces become Wei's.

266
00:16:15,370 --> 00:16:22,370
Advisors, elephants, chariots, horses
and cannons: all come under its control.

267
00:16:23,070 --> 00:16:30,070
The captured advisors
and elephants can also

268
00:16:30,640 --> 00:16:34,900
be commanded. Remember the extra
rule I mentioned for elephants?

269
00:16:35,640 --> 00:16:39,640
After Wei takes over all of Wu's pieces,

270
00:16:39,800 --> 00:16:45,010
Wu's elephants may cross the
national boundary to help defend Wei.

271
00:16:45,980 --> 00:16:50,340
If Wei is able to do so,

272
00:16:50,800 --> 00:16:54,260
it can also bring these two advisors over.

273
00:16:54,690 --> 00:16:57,710
The advisors do not walk
over one step at a time.

274
00:16:57,710 --> 00:17:00,400
We add a "palace transfer" rule instead.

275
00:17:00,460 --> 00:17:04,980
Whether Wei has only one
advisor left or still has both,

276
00:17:05,080 --> 00:17:07,880
it may want to strengthen its defence.

277
00:17:08,330 --> 00:17:11,220
It can transfer these two
advisors into its own palace.

278
00:17:11,280 --> 00:17:13,340
Use points an advisor can normally reach.

279
00:17:13,750 --> 00:17:15,510
You cannot put an advisor here,

280
00:17:15,570 --> 00:17:19,140
right? Bring it here to help defend.

281
00:17:19,240 --> 00:17:23,410
Like this. A palace transfer

282
00:17:23,510 --> 00:17:27,330
has one restriction:
it must consume a move.

283
00:17:27,690 --> 00:17:29,290
You cannot transfer an advisor

284
00:17:29,350 --> 00:17:31,493
and then move another
piece in the same turn.

285
00:17:33,980 --> 00:17:37,220
That covers using captured
advisors and elephants for defence.

286
00:17:37,630 --> 00:17:39,868
The elephants can jump
over one move at a time.

287
00:17:40,810 --> 00:17:43,130
Of course, the condition for that

288
00:17:43,350 --> 00:17:46,470
For an elephant to cross
into a neighbouring country

289
00:17:46,530 --> 00:17:52,950
and help defend, the two factions involved
must be Wu and Wei, or Wu and Shu.

290
00:17:52,950 --> 00:17:55,410
This follows taking
over the other's pieces.

291
00:17:55,450 --> 00:17:58,910
For Wei and Shu, suppose
Wei checkmates Shu.

292
00:17:58,970 --> 00:18:01,610
Shu's elephants cannot cross over,

293
00:18:01,670 --> 00:18:03,390
because Wu is enemy territory.

294
00:18:04,730 --> 00:18:10,370
But if Shu had killed the
emperor before Wei checkmated it,

295
00:18:10,370 --> 00:18:16,030
Shu's elephant could pass through
Wei... through Han territory

296
00:18:16,030 --> 00:18:17,250
to reach

297
00:18:18,020 --> 00:18:21,300
Wei's territory here. The
reverse works the same way.

298
00:18:21,750 --> 00:18:26,220
If Shu checkmates Wei,

299
00:18:26,280 --> 00:18:27,947
and Wei had killed the Han emperor,

300
00:18:29,250 --> 00:18:34,760
Wei's elephants may also
pass through Han territory

301
00:18:34,760 --> 00:18:38,000
to reach Shu. But that is a very
long journey for an elephant.

302
00:18:38,220 --> 00:18:40,330
In practice, this rule

303
00:18:41,780 --> 00:18:46,060
is somewhat questionable. You would
hardly bring an elephant all that way.

304
00:18:46,290 --> 00:18:48,750
Besides, the route is dangerous.

305
00:18:48,810 --> 00:18:50,870
The elephant might be
captured at any time.

306
00:18:53,510 --> 00:18:55,590
Finally, let's discuss winning and losing.

307
00:18:56,390 --> 00:18:58,710
There are wins, losses and draws.

308
00:18:58,770 --> 00:19:04,030
If your faction checkmates
both other factions,

309
00:19:04,090 --> 00:19:09,500
you win. If Wu first checkmates Wei,

310
00:19:09,730 --> 00:19:11,510
but is then checkmated by Shu,

311
00:19:11,570 --> 00:19:13,230
Shu is the final winner.

312
00:19:15,820 --> 00:19:18,540
If none of the three
can checkmate another,

313
00:19:18,600 --> 00:19:21,040
or if Wu checkmates Wei,

314
00:19:21,100 --> 00:19:23,148
but Wu and Shu cannot
checkmate each other,

315
00:19:24,320 --> 00:19:26,340
Wu and Shu draw,

316
00:19:26,400 --> 00:19:33,400
while Wei has lost.

317
00:19:35,130 --> 00:19:37,810
There is also something

318
00:19:39,180 --> 00:19:40,980
about alliances

319
00:19:41,400 --> 00:19:42,600
that I forgot to mention.

320
00:19:42,640 --> 00:19:46,900
Allied factions do not
give check to each other.

321
00:19:47,620 --> 00:19:53,330
For example, suppose Wu and Shu

322
00:19:53,330 --> 00:19:59,050
are allied, and Wei
gives check to Wu's king.

323
00:19:59,600 --> 00:20:00,980
The king may hide here,

324
00:20:01,060 --> 00:20:02,580
behind Shu's

325
00:20:02,640 --> 00:20:05,940
chariot. That is neither check
nor offering the king for capture.

326
00:20:07,160 --> 00:20:09,400
It is normal protection of an ally,

327
00:20:10,560 --> 00:20:12,540
or assistance with defence.

328
00:20:13,120 --> 00:20:15,800
So that position does not count as check.

329
00:20:15,860 --> 00:20:19,340
Also, if Wei's chariot pins it from here,

330
00:20:19,680 --> 00:20:22,770
Shu's chariot may only
move forward or backward.

331
00:20:22,830 --> 00:20:26,510
It cannot move sideways, since that
exposes its ally's king to capture.

332
00:20:26,970 --> 00:20:31,490
That is one restriction
after forming an alliance.

333
00:20:33,740 --> 00:20:39,080
There is also the matter of giving check.

334
00:20:39,420 --> 00:20:45,070
The same principle applies here.

335
00:20:46,120 --> 00:20:49,120
You know Chinese chess has a rule
about perpetual check, right?

336
00:20:51,360 --> 00:20:58,360
Suppose Wei repeatedly checks Wu.

337
00:20:59,940 --> 00:21:02,940
Check here, the king moves
here, then Wei checks again.

338
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:07,060
That's checkmate—wrong example.
I mean checking back and forth.

339
00:21:08,580 --> 00:21:12,720
that is pointless.

340
00:21:13,510 --> 00:21:14,790
So there is a restriction

341
00:21:14,850 --> 00:21:17,755
on perpetual check against the
player immediately before you.

342
00:21:17,880 --> 00:21:20,820
Once it reaches a
specified number of rounds,

343
00:21:20,880 --> 00:21:25,460
you can set six or nine rounds.
If it still won't change moves,

344
00:21:25,640 --> 00:21:27,180
it automatically loses.

345
00:21:28,610 --> 00:21:31,753
All its pieces are taken over by
the player immediately before it.

346
00:21:32,820 --> 00:21:36,660
But what about perpetually
checking the next player?

347
00:21:37,940 --> 00:21:39,090
For example,

348
00:21:39,620 --> 00:21:41,100
let me remove these pieces

349
00:21:43,670 --> 00:21:46,820
and put two chariots here.
If it keeps doing this...

350
00:21:47,030 --> 00:21:48,750
Suppose Wei moves here.

351
00:21:49,450 --> 00:21:51,800
[Brief phrase unclear.] It gives check,

352
00:21:51,960 --> 00:21:55,390
and the king moves. Wu checks Wei.

353
00:21:55,450 --> 00:21:56,890
After Wei responds,

354
00:21:57,260 --> 00:22:00,820
it is Shu's turn. Shu makes any move.

355
00:22:01,020 --> 00:22:03,230
Then Wu checks again,

356
00:22:03,310 --> 00:22:05,072
Wei moves again, and Shu moves again.

357
00:22:05,190 --> 00:22:10,700
Look: that has not disrupted
the normal turn order,

358
00:22:10,700 --> 00:22:14,260
has it? So perpetual check of the
previous player loses if unchanged,

359
00:22:14,320 --> 00:22:16,701
but perpetual check of the
next player is allowed.

360
00:22:17,090 --> 00:22:20,410
Checking the next player does not
disrupt the normal turn order,

361
00:22:20,510 --> 00:22:22,010
so it is allowed.

362
00:22:22,070 --> 00:22:23,220
There is also

363
00:22:40,060 --> 00:22:42,920
the matter of the board's
edges and corners.

364
00:22:44,690 --> 00:22:48,590
I have something to say about those.

365
00:22:49,280 --> 00:22:54,780
A friend once asked me this question:

366
00:22:54,880 --> 00:22:56,460
"Can this chariot move here?"

367
00:22:57,460 --> 00:23:01,940
Some boards draw the outside border
just like the playable grid lines.

368
00:23:02,350 --> 00:23:04,160
He asked, "Can the chariot move here?"

369
00:23:05,660 --> 00:23:08,210
And what about a cannon or a soldier?

370
00:23:08,630 --> 00:23:10,550
That nearly made me burst out laughing.

371
00:23:11,590 --> 00:23:14,930
I said, "How are you
moving outside the board?"

372
00:23:14,990 --> 00:23:18,130
Chariot and cannon aside,
how would a soldier get there?

373
00:23:18,190 --> 00:23:20,820
"You moved it there in
one step?" He said yes.

374
00:23:21,820 --> 00:23:24,340
I said that was wrong. This outer border

375
00:23:24,620 --> 00:23:26,960
cannot be entered by any piece.

376
00:23:28,250 --> 00:23:30,155
The horse and elephant are no exception.

377
00:23:30,350 --> 00:23:31,790
Let me remove these pieces.

378
00:23:32,090 --> 00:23:33,566
For example, with a horse here,

379
00:23:33,750 --> 00:23:36,530
it cannot jump straight over
and capture this soldier.

380
00:23:38,020 --> 00:23:39,780
As for how the horse moves,

381
00:23:40,750 --> 00:23:46,250
think of its L-shaped move as one
straight step and one diagonal step,

382
00:23:46,250 --> 00:23:52,320
two component moves. This
horse can move here, or here,

383
00:23:52,860 --> 00:23:58,370
and here or here. But it cannot move to
this point, because its straight step

384
00:23:58,370 --> 00:24:03,950
stays on a valid line, but the
diagonal step crosses this blank area.

385
00:24:03,950 --> 00:24:09,490
So that move is illegal. With
the horse here, it may move

386
00:24:09,490 --> 00:24:13,560
here, here, here and here.

387
00:24:14,920 --> 00:24:18,349
It cannot go here: both its straight
and diagonal steps leave the board.

388
00:24:18,580 --> 00:24:23,670
That is also illegal.
If the horse is here,

389
00:24:24,990 --> 00:24:27,090
it can move here or here,

390
00:24:28,020 --> 00:24:29,730
here or here,

391
00:24:30,910 --> 00:24:35,150
and here or here. At this point,
the straight step stays on the grid,

392
00:24:35,210 --> 00:24:38,020
but the diagonal step goes
outside, so that is illegal too.

393
00:24:38,210 --> 00:24:41,950
Therefore, for this piece,

394
00:24:42,170 --> 00:24:45,040
the horse, its route

395
00:24:45,780 --> 00:24:49,410
cannot cross these areas
designated for minority peoples.

396
00:24:49,470 --> 00:24:52,830
Those blank regions around the
board's corners cannot be crossed,

397
00:24:53,260 --> 00:24:55,740
whether with the straight
step or the diagonal step.

398
00:24:56,510 --> 00:24:57,990
The elephant is the same.

399
00:24:58,150 --> 00:25:02,690
I said a captured elephant could cross
the national boundary to help defend.

400
00:25:03,060 --> 00:25:04,480
It may cross this way,

401
00:25:04,540 --> 00:25:06,280
but cannot cross this other way,

402
00:25:07,180 --> 00:25:10,460
because it has the same
restriction as the horse.

403
00:25:20,010 --> 00:25:24,490
Here is another point
about winning and losing.

404
00:25:24,610 --> 00:25:26,230
Suppose, for example,

405
00:25:27,130 --> 00:25:30,310
Mid-game, I no longer want
to play. Can I resign? Yes.

406
00:25:30,410 --> 00:25:32,350
You may resign, but your pieces,

407
00:25:32,570 --> 00:25:33,790
wherever they are,

408
00:25:34,910 --> 00:25:36,060
become inactive.

409
00:25:36,520 --> 00:25:39,720
They are not taken over as
they would be after checkmate.

410
00:25:39,780 --> 00:25:41,560
They simply become inactive.

411
00:25:42,040 --> 00:25:43,520
They stay on the board,

412
00:25:43,580 --> 00:25:45,480
and neither opponent takes them over.

413
00:25:45,740 --> 00:25:48,020
Turn your king piece face down

414
00:25:48,080 --> 00:25:49,560
to indicate your resignation.

415
00:25:49,880 --> 00:25:53,213
The other two factions fight each
other or compete for control of Han.

416
00:25:53,300 --> 00:25:55,300
But your own pieces are entirely inactive.

417
00:25:59,420 --> 00:26:02,920
They can be used as obstacles,

418
00:26:05,180 --> 00:26:06,500
or, in other words,

419
00:26:06,890 --> 00:26:09,130
as cannon screens or obstacles.

420
00:26:09,190 --> 00:26:11,510
The other players may also capture them.

421
00:26:11,950 --> 00:26:13,100
They can be captured.

422
00:26:13,610 --> 00:26:16,450
They do not turn into immovable stones.

423
00:26:16,940 --> 00:26:18,090
That's not what happens.

424
00:26:28,980 --> 00:26:34,910
Now, recording a Sanguo Yan Yi Qi game.

425
00:26:34,910 --> 00:26:40,710
The board has seventeen lines

426
00:26:40,710 --> 00:26:42,930
by seventeen lines.

427
00:26:46,230 --> 00:26:48,930
Here is how its game notation works.

428
00:26:49,680 --> 00:26:51,160
First, the coordinates.

429
00:26:51,220 --> 00:26:56,770
From each side's right to left,
the files are numbered one, two,

430
00:26:56,770 --> 00:27:00,350
three, four, five, six, seven,
eight, nine, up to seventeen.

431
00:27:01,100 --> 00:27:06,170
Each side does this. For Wu, the
chariots are on files five and thirteen.

432
00:27:06,290 --> 00:27:10,760
The central file is nine;
the king starts on file nine.

433
00:27:12,690 --> 00:27:17,100
Its files one through four
correspond to Wei's side,

434
00:27:18,270 --> 00:27:20,413
and fourteen through
seventeen to Shu's side.

435
00:27:20,910 --> 00:27:25,070
If Wu kills the Han emperor,

436
00:27:25,960 --> 00:27:29,560
the four Han pieces use Wu's
coordinates for their moves,

437
00:27:29,620 --> 00:27:32,300
rather than Han's. Han has
no coordinates of its own.

438
00:27:32,640 --> 00:27:34,926
Remember: Han has no
separate coordinate system.

439
00:27:35,600 --> 00:27:37,200
Whoever kills the emperor,

440
00:27:37,990 --> 00:27:41,540
the three Han chariots and cannon
use that faction's coordinates.

441
00:27:41,600 --> 00:27:46,170
For Wu, after killing the emperor,
these are files seven, nine and eleven.

442
00:27:46,740 --> 00:27:49,580
For Wei, they are files four and three.

443
00:27:50,120 --> 00:27:52,263
For Shu, they are files
fourteen and fifteen.

444
00:27:52,850 --> 00:27:59,280
Keep that in mind. Now suppose

445
00:27:59,920 --> 00:28:02,860
these two Wei cannons are
both on the same file,

446
00:28:02,920 --> 00:28:04,880
which is file ten from Wei's perspective.

447
00:28:05,580 --> 00:28:08,720
If you want to move the rear cannon,

448
00:28:09,190 --> 00:28:12,870
write "Rear cannon,
sideways to file seven."

449
00:28:15,780 --> 00:28:20,910
They share a file. "Cannon on
file 10 to 7"—which cannon?

450
00:28:21,100 --> 00:28:28,100
Similarly, after Wei
kills the emperor, suppose

451
00:28:28,520 --> 00:28:34,440
you move the middle cannon: "Middle cannon
to file ten"—actually, "to file seven."

452
00:28:36,960 --> 00:28:43,130
Here is an even more extreme case:
after Wei kills the emperor,

453
00:28:43,130 --> 00:28:47,290
all five chariots line up on one
file. How do you distinguish them?

454
00:28:47,750 --> 00:28:52,810
Use one, two, three, four and five;
distinguish them by number.

455
00:28:52,870 --> 00:28:56,390
Lower numbers mean farther forward;
higher numbers mean farther back.

456
00:28:56,820 --> 00:28:59,890
For example: "4th chariot,
sideways to file 9,"

457
00:29:00,670 --> 00:29:03,870
not "Chariot on file 4 to file
9"—which of the five chariots?

458
00:29:04,330 --> 00:29:11,330
Right? There is also another possibility,

459
00:29:12,400 --> 00:29:14,160
which can occur in theory,

460
00:29:14,220 --> 00:29:15,440
but not in actual play.

461
00:29:15,910 --> 00:29:18,270
If Wei both kills the
emperor and defeats Wu,

462
00:29:19,230 --> 00:29:21,430
all seven chariots are on file 4.

463
00:29:21,470 --> 00:29:23,710
Of course, that is completely unrealistic.

464
00:29:23,770 --> 00:29:26,870
But to explain how moves are recorded,

465
00:29:27,150 --> 00:29:31,930
I'll include it: number them one,
two, three, four, five, six and seven.

466
00:29:32,030 --> 00:29:33,910
The same principle applies:

467
00:29:34,170 --> 00:29:37,110
lower numbers are farther forward,
higher numbers farther back.

468
00:29:37,170 --> 00:29:38,590
If this chariot moves here,

469
00:29:38,650 --> 00:29:40,840
write "Sixth chariot,
sideways to file seven,"

470
00:29:41,170 --> 00:29:42,730
because it is sixth in the line.

471
00:29:49,990 --> 00:29:55,520
Also, the creator mentioned
earlier [name unclear]

472
00:29:55,520 --> 00:29:59,900
suggested in a video

473
00:30:00,420 --> 00:30:02,753
that each full round should
represent one season.

474
00:30:02,820 --> 00:30:07,610
I think that is quite good.
Let me explain why.

475
00:30:08,050 --> 00:30:11,590
First, I strongly oppose having
one round represent one month.

476
00:30:11,650 --> 00:30:15,570
That would make twelve rounds a year.

477
00:30:16,980 --> 00:30:21,580
One move each by Wu, Wei and
Shu would count as a month,

478
00:30:22,260 --> 00:30:24,060
and twelve moves each as a year.

479
00:30:24,120 --> 00:30:29,670
The longest game of this variant
I've seen lasted 117 rounds.

480
00:30:29,670 --> 00:30:34,160
That would settle the Three Kingdoms
conflict in under ten years.

481
00:30:36,740 --> 00:30:38,280
Ten years? Never mind ten;

482
00:30:38,340 --> 00:30:41,040
the Three Kingdoms war was
still going after thirty years.

483
00:30:41,100 --> 00:30:45,620
So one round per month
is entirely unreasonable.

484
00:30:46,280 --> 00:30:47,560
Now consider one season.

485
00:30:47,620 --> 00:30:49,220
With one season per full round,

486
00:30:49,260 --> 00:30:52,240
four rounds make a year.

487
00:30:53,180 --> 00:30:57,800
Again, using a maximum of 120 rounds,

488
00:30:58,340 --> 00:30:59,880
in less than thirty years

489
00:30:59,960 --> 00:31:02,020
the war would already be over.

490
00:31:02,690 --> 00:31:04,071
Isn't that still rather slow?

491
00:31:05,480 --> 00:31:08,540
Starting in AD 220 would
bring us to AD 250,

492
00:31:08,940 --> 00:31:10,845
when Shu had not even fallen yet, right?

493
00:31:12,580 --> 00:31:16,740
The conflict began with the
Yellow Turban Rebellion in AD 184,

494
00:31:16,800 --> 00:31:19,370
and ended in AD 280, when
Western Jin conquered Wu.

495
00:31:19,530 --> 00:31:21,030
That is ninety-six years.

496
00:31:21,660 --> 00:31:24,240
Ninety-six years corresponding
to ninety-six rounds

497
00:31:24,300 --> 00:31:25,840
would be ideal.

498
00:31:27,140 --> 00:31:29,450
So whether it is a season

499
00:31:29,510 --> 00:31:32,930
or a month per round, neither is
as good as one year per round.

500
00:31:33,310 --> 00:31:35,400
In other words, Wu makes one move,

501
00:31:35,640 --> 00:31:37,688
Wei makes one move,
and Shu makes one move.

502
00:31:37,810 --> 00:31:40,950
That completes one round,

503
00:31:41,170 --> 00:31:45,710
which represents one year. There is also

504
00:31:46,310 --> 00:31:49,650
the official convention
for recording moves.

505
00:31:49,710 --> 00:31:52,310
They begin in AD 189,

506
00:31:52,370 --> 00:31:54,310
the year of Dong Zhuo's upheaval.

507
00:31:56,100 --> 00:31:58,400
I don't think that is a good choice. Why?

508
00:31:58,910 --> 00:32:02,170
The conflict should begin in AD 184.

509
00:32:02,230 --> 00:32:04,720
Without the Yellow Turban Rebellion,

510
00:32:04,780 --> 00:32:07,780
there would not have been the
later regional separatist powers.

511
00:32:08,310 --> 00:32:11,470
Without those powers, there would
be no Three Kingdoms period.

512
00:32:11,650 --> 00:32:15,680
So its roots should be traced to AD 184,

513
00:32:15,740 --> 00:32:17,788
rather than Dong Zhuo's
upheaval in AD 189.

514
00:32:21,960 --> 00:32:25,340
That is about it for the rules.

515
00:32:26,710 --> 00:32:29,450
Now I'll introduce a
few small tactical tips.

516
00:32:29,530 --> 00:32:35,140
One famous tactic in this game is
"killing with a borrowed knife."

517
00:32:35,140 --> 00:32:39,590
Let me give another example.

518
00:32:42,310 --> 00:32:45,350
Wei jumps its horse here.

519
00:32:45,890 --> 00:32:47,950
After Wei moves, it is Shu's turn.

520
00:32:48,250 --> 00:32:50,980
Shu can capture that horse.

521
00:32:51,470 --> 00:32:53,375
That is "killing with a borrowed knife."

522
00:32:55,880 --> 00:32:58,280
Also: two birds with one stone,

523
00:32:58,340 --> 00:33:03,920
or three birds with one arrow.
Those are particularly complex,

524
00:33:03,920 --> 00:33:05,893
so I won't explain them further. Instead,

525
00:33:05,893 --> 00:33:08,491
let's discuss one chariot
defending against one horse.

526
00:33:10,120 --> 00:33:13,110
If this horse wants to kill the emperor,

527
00:33:13,910 --> 00:33:16,243
the best piece to defend
against it is a chariot.

528
00:33:17,940 --> 00:33:20,060
For example, if the
horse reaches this point,

529
00:33:20,120 --> 00:33:22,120
the best position for the chariot is here,

530
00:33:22,530 --> 00:33:25,810
because it can cover both of
the horse's attacking routes.

531
00:33:26,730 --> 00:33:29,670
But if the horse jumps here,

532
00:33:30,150 --> 00:33:32,130
one chariot cannot stop it.

533
00:33:32,190 --> 00:33:34,070
If the chariot watches this route,

534
00:33:34,250 --> 00:33:36,290
it can cover this point, but not that one.

535
00:33:36,630 --> 00:33:38,850
You need two chariots stationed here.

536
00:33:40,370 --> 00:33:41,950
Pay particular attention to that.

537
00:33:43,020 --> 00:33:46,260
Remember the pattern of a square
divided into four smaller squares.

538
00:33:46,320 --> 00:33:50,670
When the horse and
chariot form that pattern,

539
00:33:50,750 --> 00:33:52,590
the horse cannot reach this point,

540
00:33:52,650 --> 00:33:53,810
or even this point.

541
00:33:53,870 --> 00:33:59,190
It can only retreat. So,

542
00:34:00,700 --> 00:34:03,420
with the horse here,
one chariot cannot defend.

543
00:34:03,480 --> 00:34:04,800
But if it is here,

544
00:34:05,000 --> 00:34:06,180
here, here or here,

545
00:34:06,720 --> 00:34:08,380
one chariot can defend.

546
00:34:08,620 --> 00:34:10,380
If the horse jumps here,

547
00:34:10,440 --> 00:34:12,821
the chariot can stand here.
The same applies here.

548
00:34:13,880 --> 00:34:15,300
When the horse jumps here,

549
00:34:15,360 --> 00:34:17,100
the chariot can defend from here.

550
00:34:18,370 --> 00:34:22,620
And also here. That's how one chariot
prevents a horse from killing the emperor.

551
00:34:24,430 --> 00:34:26,150
Of course, a horse

552
00:34:26,210 --> 00:34:28,810
that jumps into this Han area

553
00:34:29,050 --> 00:34:33,280
will destabilise the position,
so whichever faction it belongs to,

554
00:34:33,360 --> 00:34:35,074
if it casually jumps its horse here,

555
00:34:35,420 --> 00:34:38,420
it will very quickly face defensive
action from both opponents.

556
00:34:38,890 --> 00:34:40,190
That is inevitable.

557
00:34:40,510 --> 00:34:42,290
After the horse kills the emperor,

558
00:34:42,650 --> 00:34:48,020
those three chariots and one
cannon are truly frightening,

559
00:34:48,080 --> 00:34:52,210
aren't they? They are a
very intimidating force.

560
00:34:52,490 --> 00:34:53,830
So that horse,

561
00:34:54,200 --> 00:34:57,080
wherever it goes near Han,

562
00:34:57,140 --> 00:35:00,854
must be guarded against, even at the
cost of sacrificing a chariot to stop it.

563
00:35:03,440 --> 00:35:07,210
Another example:

564
00:35:07,950 --> 00:35:11,730
after an alliance, how should
the third faction defend?

565
00:35:12,420 --> 00:35:16,540
On its next move, the horse can
fork both chariots, like this.

566
00:35:17,250 --> 00:35:22,960
It can even trade the horse for a
chariot. So, once an alliance forms,

567
00:35:24,470 --> 00:35:26,770
for example, an alliance with Shu,

568
00:35:26,830 --> 00:35:30,070
on Wei's next turn it must
protect these two chariots.

569
00:35:30,810 --> 00:35:34,539
I suggest moving this chariot
well away. Anywhere will do,

570
00:35:34,539 --> 00:35:36,660
to avoid a fork [pieces unclear].

571
00:35:37,950 --> 00:35:39,807
If you move this other chariot instead,

572
00:35:40,360 --> 00:35:44,074
after taking the cannon it still forks
two chariots; this one cannot be saved.

573
00:35:46,770 --> 00:35:51,900
Similarly, when Wu and
Wei form an alliance,

574
00:35:51,960 --> 00:35:57,300
Shu also has these two chariots to
protect; move this one well away.

575
00:35:58,090 --> 00:36:00,470
Then the horse cannot fork both chariots.

576
00:36:01,410 --> 00:36:03,850
There is one special case, however.

577
00:36:05,600 --> 00:36:09,180
If Shu allies with Wu in this position,
you can ignore the horse here.

578
00:36:09,590 --> 00:36:13,850
Look: two of those square patterns
completely contain the horse.

579
00:36:19,590 --> 00:36:22,638
That concludes my introduction
to the rules of Sanguo Yan Yi Qi.

580
00:36:23,090 --> 00:36:24,290
I'll finish here.

581
00:36:24,960 --> 00:36:28,960
I had recorded some earlier videos,

582
00:36:29,020 --> 00:36:30,400
but deleted all of them.

583
00:36:30,810 --> 00:36:33,050
I'm very sorry about that.

584
00:36:33,610 --> 00:36:35,790
This video was also prompted by

585
00:36:36,140 --> 00:36:41,000
the great enthusiasm of that
Bilibili creator [name unclear].

586
00:36:41,060 --> 00:36:43,740
So I remade the tutorial.

587
00:36:44,420 --> 00:36:46,620
Please forgive any shortcomings.

588
00:36:47,380 --> 00:36:48,760
That's all for this video.
